#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079450} :activity/id #uuid "fa63b04f-e0ce-592f-b214-495b5c1b67a9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050566 :topic/id #uuid "546b628e-ba40-5207-8646-07c283c27604" :topic/math-academy-id 631} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079450 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079616 :tutorial/content "A **function** is a rule where *every* input value is assigned *precisely one* output value.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the rule defined by\n\n$$\nf(x) = x+3\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ can be any number from the **set** $\\{1,2,3,4\\}$. A set is a collection of numbers we can use in our rule.\n\nLet's calculate the possible values of $f(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(1) &= 1 + 3 = 4 \\\\\nf(2) &= 2 + 3 = 5 \\\\\nf(3) &= 3 + 3 = 6 \\\\\nf(4) &= 4 + 3 = 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this rule using a **mapping diagram**, like the one shown below.\n\n![](images/47/4714001592222932934cb7edd5852542a2e990e6680daad85e2db88b9c7cef47.png)\n\nThis rule *is* a function because *every* input is mapped to *precisely one* output." :tutorial/id #uuid "88ebc343-41ab-500a-8668-451bbf36ec8a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1488 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "93acbf1d-7504-5269-982c-7785e36bfa3d" :step/math-academy-id 3581 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079452}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079451 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078527 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078142 :question/id #uuid "6d57991d-4cdb-546b-80c5-4df14f1b385d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1096"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cc2167e-d318-54d5-8b67-47afbc65f530" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048652}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079180}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Rule Given by a Mapping Diagram Is a Function"} :step/id #uuid "953349df-46b8-543b-a772-87c3e0ffd977" :step/math-academy-id 3585 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079454}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079452 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079694 :tutorial/content "Now let's consider the rule given by the following mapping diagram:\n\n![](images/b0/b02a511f8043bb7e39df2de082befa267f795948ea6c1cc1ef38eb9df4a8cb1d.png)\n\nThis rule is *not* a function because *at least one* input is assigned to *more than one* output. In this case, the input $x=1$ is mapped to *both* $2$ and $-2$.\n\nFinally, let's consider the rule given by the following mapping diagram:\n\n![](images/be/bea4ee8835a6111e105670863edede9450ad26dbfbea631454d5c8d6cd76a6a1.png)\n\nThis rule is *not* a function because *at least one* input is not assigned to an output. In this case, the input $0$ is not assigned an output." :tutorial/id #uuid "f58c4e80-86e3-5b65-99a3-2a16208231e4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5913 :tutorial/title "Examples of Rules That Are Not Functions"} :step/id #uuid "4034d183-7b91-5f03-a1ca-b71ce2a3ea8c" :step/math-academy-id 36150 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079455}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079453 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078528 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078381 :question/id #uuid "279a2ac1-2577-5ec3-8eb4-8496650c6b54" :question/math-academy-id "e-9905"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b869d240-38f8-52c8-bb87-110d7ac616b8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078955}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Rule Given by a Table Is a Function"} :step/id #uuid "d65897aa-199d-5bb6-a05a-f5877fe63002" :step/math-academy-id 37242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079454 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079695 :tutorial/content "When a rule is represented as a table, we have a function if and only if all values in the *first row* are *distinct* (i.e., different).\n\nLet's consider some examples.\n\nThe rule defined by the following table is a function. Notice that all values in the first row are distinct. Every input value is assigned to exactly one output value.\n\n| **$x$** | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $0$ | $7$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nOn the other hand, the rule defined by the following table is *not* a function. Notice that the first row contains the value $\\color{red}3$ twice. The input value $3$ is assigned to two output values ($0$ and $-1$).\n\n| **$x$** | $1$ | $2$ | $\\color{red}3$ | $4$ | $\\color{red}3$ | $6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $0$ | $7$ | $-1$ | $5$ |" :tutorial/id #uuid "f5d2e3d4-e985-565a-81f8-c8b8e93dc73d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6156 :tutorial/title "Rules Represented Using Tables"} :step/id #uuid "53c16c15-06ad-566b-bb58-7baf803dbf46" :step/math-academy-id 37341 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079453}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079455 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079696 :tutorial/content "So, we must remember that a rule is a function if and only if *every* input is mapped to *precisely one* output.\n\nIn particular, a rule is *not* a function in the following cases:\n\n- An input value is not assigned an output value. For example, the following situation is *not allowed* for functions (an input value with no arrow from it):\n\n![](images/bb/bb53865d210d631d78e5ce48aa96ecff99da8995936a085c789c3471ffb9e6a0.png)\n\n- An input value is assigned to more than one output value. For example, the following situation is *not allowed* for functions (an input value with more than one arrow from it):\n\n![](images/18/18831dc73e561faa69748fed111eb7e1024d35849fb6624594ae08d8c35301ee.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "f92a951f-7b57-51b4-b130-bf0d4c0da4a5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6161 :tutorial/title "A Summary"} :step/id #uuid "e2bd4f1a-fe61-532e-a324-4c924a280b8e" :step/math-academy-id 37364 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079451}}] :activity/title "Visual Representations of Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078655}]}
